{
  "version": "2026-10-29.1",
  "status": "approved 2026-10-29 (D-031) under the project owner's instruction; reviewed against the real recipe data, not certified. Not a regulatory or completeness claim.",
  "scope": "The 9 allergen codes from decision A-08 (unchanged). Not medical advice; cross-contact is not modelled. Flags mean 'contains or may contain'.",
  "how_it_is_used": [
    "synonyms: whole-phrase rewrites applied while normalizing, before units and prep words are removed (e.g. 'gram flour' -> 'chickpea flour'). Exact identities only; no fuzzy or substring matching.",
    "keywords: any ingredient whose normalized name contains a keyword (whole-word match) gets that allergen flag. Keywords only ADD flags; they never make a recipe's allergen data complete.",
    "keyword_exceptions: phrases that contain a keyword but are not that allergen (e.g. coconut milk is not dairy). Phrases are written in normalized form.",
    "known: single foods whose allergen codes are known, keyed by their normalized name. A recipe is allergen-complete only if EVERY ingredient matches a 'known' entry exactly. Ambiguous products that are still useful (generic oil, vinegar, baking powder) list every allergen they may contain. Composite or variable products (bread, pasta, stock, sauces, spice blends, chocolate, mayonnaise) are deliberately absent, so recipes using them stay 'unknown' and are excluded for users with allergen exclusions."
  ],
