  "update_process": [
    "Edit this file, bump 'version', and run `python -m pytest -q tests/test_ingredients.py` (every key must already be in normalized form, every code must be one of the 9 approved codes, every synonym must point at a known entry).",
    "Check the effect with `python scripts/import_recipes.py --dry-run` (allergen_complete per source), then re-import so stored recipes pick up the change. Record the decision in docs/implementation/DECISIONS_AND_BLOCKERS.md.",
    "Only add a 'known' entry for a single food whose allergen status does not vary by brand; when unsure, leave it out (unknown) or list every allergen it may contain."
  ],
  "notes_for_review": [
    "Highly refined oils are exempt from allergen labelling in the US and EU, but an unnamed 'oil' cannot be told apart from cold-pressed groundnut or sesame oil, so it carries those flags.",
    "'flour' is treated as wheat flour (gluten). 'soy sauce' stays known as soy + gluten: every variant contains at most those. 'coconut' is flagged tree_nuts as a conservative choice. Oats are flagged gluten (EU lists oats as a cereal containing gluten; contamination is common).",
    "Ingredient names that are dropped entirely by normalization (e.g. whole 'cloves', where 'clove' is also a unit word) produce an empty key and therefore stay unknown."
  ]
}
